cantagnos was a typical rural village of  a few hundred families  it had rice fields a church and a school  now it's the scene of some of the  biggest devastation in the philippine  it sounded like an explosion we ran up  to the hill to see what was going on it  was awful we could hear people crying  calling out for  help tropical storm maggie brought  incessant rains in the eastern visayas  region many areas have lost power and  communication lines  but cantagnos was the most devastated  survivors here tell us there were two  landslides the first one was at around 5  a.m which gave some time for some  families to evacuate but the second one  they say they saw how waves of mud came  rolling down the mountains burying  hundreds of civilians this is all what's  left of it now  on the other side of the hill four  bodies have been pulled out from the  rubble  government responders are doing the best  they can  but their efforts have been hampered by  more rain the mud is at least five  meters deep so they will have to wait  before they can bring in heavy equipment  all visible  we could only get bodies in the  periphery because it is dangerous even  for our men i think this process of  retrieval will take time  flowers and incense are offered and the  souls  all  the peoples  and the prayer for those who remain  buried  and unaccounted for  lent is meant to be a week of gratitude  and grace for millions of filipino  families but for the survivors of the  landslide in cantagnos this period will  now remind him of their overwhelming  grief  jamal allendogen al jazeera bye bye lady  province central philippines
 

